WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 192: George H.W. Bush: The Youngest Aviator, Chichi Jima, and the Presidency (1941–1989)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.520
[Rebecca] Today we step into the extraordinary, honorable world of George Herbert Walker Bush.

4
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:23.360
[Rebecca] Before he was president, 41 was a teenage combat pilot who survived being shot down in World War II, a Texas oil wildcatter, and the most versatile public servant of his generation.

5
00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:29.360
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of Chichijima, the oil patch, and the road to the White House.

6
00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:52.240
[George] His father, Prescott Bush, was a partner in Brown Brothers Harriman and later a respected United States Senator from Connecticut.

7
00:00:52.240 --> 00:01:05.440
[George] His mother, Dorothy Walker Bush, was a formidable, devout woman who instilled in her sons an absolute horror of bragging or self-promotion, constantly admonishing them to put others first and practice modesty.

8
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:18.240
[George] In June 1942, on the day he graduated from the Elite Phillips Academy in Andover, Massachusetts, 18-year-old George listened to the graduation address by Secretary of War Henry Stimson.

9
00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:25.360
[George] Despite having been accepted into Yale, Bush walked straight into the Navy recruiting office on his 18th birthday.

10
00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:40.320
[George] Enlisting as a seaman second class, he completed grueling flight training in 10 months, receiving his naval aviator wings and an ensign's commission at age 18, the youngest commissioned combat pilot in the entire United States Navy.

11
00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:47.520
[George] Enlisting on his 18th birthday demonstrated the deep patrician sense of noblesse obliged that defined the Bush family.

12
00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:52.400
[George] Privilege conferred a solemn duty to serve one's country in its hour of peril.

13
00:01:52.400 --> 00:02:00.920
[George] His decision to enlist at 18 set the pattern for a lifetime of public service, always choosing the path of duty over personal privilege.

14
00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:10.040
[George] That youthful decision to serve his country set the tone for a life of selfless dedication to the highest ideals of American Republican leadership.

15
00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:26.200
[George] Assigned to Torpedo Squadron 51 aboard the light aircraft carrier USS San Jacinto in the Pacific, Lieutenant Bush flew 58 combat missions in his Grumman TBM Avenger, which he affectionately christened Barbara after his teenage sweetheart.

16
00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:41.000
[George] On the morning of September 2, 1944, Bush led a four-plane flight to bomb a heavily fortified Japanese radio and radar installation on the volcanic island of Chichai Jima, 150 miles north of Iwo Jima.

17
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:47.240
[George] Diving through intense, black anti-aircraft fire, Bush's plane was struck by heavy flak.

18
00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:53.000
[George] The engine exploded in roaring flames, filling the cockpit with thick, toxic smoke.

19
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:06.200
[George] Refusing to pull out of his dive, 20-year-old Bush stayed on target, released four 500-pound bombs directly onto the radio installation, and pulled out over the ocean before ordering his crew to bail out.

20
00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:12.520
[George] Parachuting into the waves, Bush suffered a head laceration when he struck the horizontal stabilizer.

21
00:03:12.520 --> 00:03:27.960
[George] Floating in a tiny yellow rubber life raft four miles offshore, Bush watched in terror as Japanese patrol boats set out from the island to capture him, paddling frantically with his hands while American fighter planes strafed the enemy boats to keep them at bay.

22
00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:39.080
[George] The harrowing combat mission over Chichai Jima revealed the extraordinary valor of a 20 year old pilot who completed his bombing run while his aircraft was consumed by roaring flames.

23
00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:48.880
[George] His courage in the skies over Chichai Jima exemplified the selfless heroism of the greatest generation, young men who risked everything to preserve democratic freedom.

24
00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:56.640
[George] Floating in the vast expanse of the ocean, Bush drew upon a profound faith that sustained his courage through hours of peril.

25
00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:10.720
[George] For three agonizing hours, Bush drifted alone under a scorching sun, vomiting from swallowed saltwater, weeping for his two lost crewmen, radioman John Delaney and gunner William White, who had perished in the crash.

26
00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:21.920
[George] Suddenly, through his tears, he saw a black steel hull rise like a sea monster from the waves, the submarine USS Finback, which had surfaced right beside his raft.

27
00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:26.880
[George] Looking up at the bearded submariners, Bush stammered, happy to be aboard.

28
00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:35.600
[George] A sailor aboard the submarine captured the rescue on 16-millimeter color film, one of the most remarkable wartime footage reels in history.

29
00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:38.240
[George] Unknown to Bush until decades later.

30
00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:41.520
[George] His survival was an unbelievable miracle of fate.

31
00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:54.240
[George] Eight other American aviators shot down during the same raid were captured by Japanese troops on Chichai Jima, subjected to horrific torture, executed, and their livers cannibalized by Japanese officers.

32
00:04:54.240 --> 00:05:07.040
[George] For thirty days, Bush served aboard the Finback as it patrolled submarine waters, awarded the distinguished Flying Cross for his heroism, haunted for the rest of his life by a quiet, reverent question.

33
00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.640
[George] Why was I spared?

34
00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.200
[George] And what did God have in store for me?

35
00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:21.920
[George] That miraculous submarine rescue by the Finback forged in Bush an abiding sense of providential purpose, believing his life had been spared for a greater destiny of public service.

36
00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:31.800
[George] That miraculous rescue at sea gave Bush an unshakable inner serenity, viewing every subsequent day as an unearned gift to be spent in service to others.

37
00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:45.640
[George] Returning home in January 1945, Bush married 18-year-old Barbara Pierce in Rye, New York, beginning a magnificent, 73-year love story that became the longest marriage in presidential history.

38
00:05:45.640 --> 00:06:01.240
[George] Entering Yale University, Bush captained the varsity baseball team as a slick fielding first baseman, leading the Bulldogs to the College World Series in 1947 and 1948, proudly meeting baseball legend Babe Ruth at home plate.

39
00:06:01.240 --> 00:06:08.680
[George] Graduating Phi Beta Kappa with an economics degree in two and a half years, Bush made a bold, independent choice.

40
00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:14.680
[George] Rejecting comfortable Wall Street banking jobs, he packed his wife and baby son George W.

41
00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:22.360
[George] into a red studebaker and drove 1,500 miles to the sun-baked, dusty oil patch of Odessa, Texas.

42
00:06:22.360 --> 00:06:32.200
[George] Starting out as a humble oilfield equipment clerk, sweeping warehouse floors for dresser industries, Bush co-founded Zapata Petroleum in Midland, Texas.

43
00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:41.800
[George] Pioneering offshore drilling technology in the Gulf of Mexico and amassing an independent fortune through sheer grit, fair dealing, and mutual trust.

44
00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:52.920
[George] The move to the Permian Basin proved Bush's independent spirit, building a thriving business from scratch through integrity, teamwork, and sweat rather than family connections.

45
00:06:52.920 --> 00:07:01.800
[George] His success in the Texas oil business proved that integrity, fairness, and hard work are the truest foundations of entrepreneurial prosperity.

46
00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:11.320
[George] Driven by his mother's ethos of selfless civic duty, Bush entered Texas politics, winning election to Congress from Houston in 1966.

47
00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:18.160
[George] Over the next two decades, Bush compiled the most astonishing and varied resume in modern American statecraft.

48
00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:28.880
[George] In 1971, President Nixon appointed him United States Ambassador to the United Nations, where Bush's warmth and diplomatic grace won friends across the globe.

49
00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:36.720
[George] In 1973, in the depths of the Watergate scandal, Nixon asked Bush to chair the Republican National Committee.

50
00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:44.080
[George] Bush preserved the party's integrity, eventually confronting Nixon directly and urging him to resign for the good of the nation.

51
00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:48.560
[George] In 1974, President Ford appointed Bush chief of the U.S.

52
00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:58.240
[George] Liaison office in Beijing, where George and Barbara rode bicycles through the streets of the Chinese Capitol, building personal ties with Chinese leaders.

53
00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:10.560
[George] In 1976, Ford summoned Bush home to serve as director of Central Intelligence, restoring morale and ethical standards to a demoralized CIA in the wake of congressional investigations.

54
00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:21.920
[George] His extraordinary breadth of public service, from the halls of Congress and the United Nations to Beijing and the CIA, gave Bush an unrivaled preparation for the highest office in the land.

55
00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:32.080
[George] Throughout his varied diplomatic and intelligence careers, Bush was revered by colleagues for his quiet discretion, modesty, and deep institutional loyalty.

56
00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:44.720
[George] In 1980, after challenging Ronald Reagan for the Republican presidential nomination in a spirited primary campaign, Bush accepted Reagan's invitation to become his vice presidential running mate.

57
00:08:44.720 --> 00:09:01.400
[George] For eight years, Bush served as the quintessential loyal team player, traveling over one million miles to 74 countries, representing America at state funerals, and providing trusted, discreet counsel in the Oval Office without a single public leak or disagreement.

58
00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:11.960
[George] When Reagan was shot in 1981, Bush deliberately refused to land his helicopter on the South Lawn, insisting that only the president should land on the White House grounds.

59
00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:17.960
[George] In August 1988, accepting the Republican presidential nomination in New Orleans.

60
00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:24.760
[George] Bush delivered the speech of his life, penned by Peggy Noonan, I want a kinder and gentler nation.

61
00:09:24.760 --> 00:09:35.000
[George] He praised the power of voluntary community service as a thousand points of light, and made an unyielding, fateful promise, read my lips, no new taxes.

62
00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:44.520
[George] In November, Bush defeated Massachusetts Governor Michael Dukakis in a 40-state landslide, winning 426 electoral votes.

63
00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:56.280
[George] His eight years as vice president established the gold standard of executive loyalty, providing discrete, dependable counsel to Ronald Reagan while representing the nation across the globe.

64
00:09:56.280 --> 00:10:05.800
[George] His partnership with Ronald Reagan established a model of vice presidential loyalty, providing trusted counsel without ever seeking the public spotlight.

65
00:10:05.800 --> 00:10:17.240
[George] On January 20, 1989, 64-year-old George Herbert Walker Bush took the oath of office as the 41st president of the United States on the west front of the Capitol.

66
00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:23.720
[George] He was the first sitting vice president elected to the presidency since Martin Van Buren in 1836.

67
00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:28.200
[George] In his inaugural address, Bush spoke with characteristic humility.

68
00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:33.320
[George] We meet on democracy's front porch, a good place to talk as neighbors and friends.

69
00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:35.480
[George] We have more will than wallet.

70
00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:37.400
[George] But will is what we need.

71
00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:43.320
[George] He took command at the most perilous and turbulent geopolitical moment since the end of World War I.I.

72
00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:51.600
[George] The Soviet Empire was cracking at the seams, Eastern Europe was stirring with rebellion, and the postwar order was about to dissolve.

73
00:10:51.600 --> 00:11:05.600
[George] With four decades of military, diplomatic, and executive experience, the quiet aviator from Chichai Jima stood ready to navigate the ship of state through a historic global transformation with steady, understated mastery.

74
00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:09.840
[George] Taking the presidential oath in 1989, George H.W.

75
00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:16.800
[George] Bush brought a lifetime of seasoned judgment and quiet modesty to the helm of a rapidly transforming world.

76
00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:26.480
[George] President Bush brought an air of quiet dignity and seasoned statesmanship to the White House, navigating historic global changes with calm wisdom.

77
00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:27.600
[Rebecca] George H.

78
00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:27.840
[Rebecca] W.

79
00:11:27.840 --> 00:11:34.080
[Rebecca] Bush reached the presidency through a lifetime of extraordinary personal heroism and humble public service.

80
00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:38.000
[Rebecca] But his greatest tests lay immediately ahead on the world stage.

81
00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:41.760
[Rebecca] Next week, we turn to the second half of the Bush presidency.

82
00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:55.040
[Rebecca] His masterly handling of the fall of the Berlin Wall, the rapid triumph of Operation Desert Storm, his famous White House ban on broccoli that ignited a vegetable war, and the gracious farewell note left for Bill Clinton.

83
00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:59.280
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

